WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:06.383
Tokens, chunks and MCP configs: the small files behind AI apps.

2
00:00:07.883 --> 00:00:13.846
How tokens are counted, how to cut documents for RAG, what makes a fine-tuning

3
00:00:13.846 --> 00:00:19.427
file valid, what is inside a GGUF model, and how to write MCP configs for

4
00:00:19.427 --> 00:00:20.192
every app.

5
00:00:21.592 --> 00:00:25.816
Tokens are not words. Models do not read letters or words.

6
00:00:27.116 --> 00:00:32.360
Chunking documents for RAG. Retrieval augmented generation, or RAG, means

7
00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:37.891
finding the few passages of your documents that answer a question and putting

8
00:00:37.891 --> 00:00:39.831
only those into the prompt.

9
00:00:41.131 --> 00:00:47.254
What is inside a model file. Local models come as GGUF files for llama.cpp,

10
00:00:47.254 --> 00:00:52.480
Ollama and LM Studio, or as safetensors files from Hugging Face.

11
00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:56.364
MCP configs without the guesswork.

12
00:00:56.364 --> 00:01:01.760
The Model Context Protocol lets an assistant use tools you run, such as

13
00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:04.724
a file system, a browser or a database.

14
00:01:06.024 --> 00:01:09.046
To try it, open Token Counter in OpenFile.

15
00:01:09.046 --> 00:01:13.148
Drop in a file, adjust the settings and press the button.

16
00:01:13.148 --> 00:01:16.819
It all runs on your device, so nothing is uploaded.

17
00:01:19.124 --> 00:01:23.561
Read the full post, with live demos, on the OpenFile blog.
